# G096 — RPG Character Stat Creator

*v2. See `rpg-character.md` for prose: class as data (multiplier
table), derived stats computed never stored, seeded RNG for cross-
language determinism, point budget with per-class caps as database-
style invariants, line-based save format.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Character Gallery

[vault-character-gallery @gallery-root
    IN ORDER TO
        load every saved character from the gallery directory
        present a roster view, let the user roll new characters
        and persist level-ups immediately so the sheet is always latest

    @files      <- [fs[ls {path: @gallery-root}]]
    @characters <- for @file in @files
        @text <- [fs[read-string {path: @file:path}]]
        [rpg[parse-character {text: @text}]]

    [ui[render-gallery {characters: @characters}]]

    sync @user-events {
        new @name @class ->
            @c <- [rpg[roll
                         {name: @name, class: @class,
                          seed: [clock[now]]}]]
            [fs[write-string
                 {path: @gallery-root + "/" + @name + ".char",
                  content: [rpg[to-text {character: @c}]]}]]

        level-up @name ->
            @c <- @characters where name is @name first
            [rpg[level-up {character: @c}]]
            [fs[write-string
                 {path: @gallery-root + "/" + @name + ".char",
                  content: [rpg[to-text {character: @c}]]}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[rpg
    roll                 {name, class, seed}
    adjusted             {character, stat}
    derived              {character}
    level-up             {character}
    spend-point          {character, stat, points}    ;; || over-budget | over-cap
    to-text              {character}
    parse-character      {text}                        ;; || parse-error
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: class as table, dispatched by `where`

[rpg[adjusted {character: @c, stat: @s}]
    IN ORDER TO
        apply the class multiplier without a code branch per class
        so adding a Bard is adding a row, not a module

    @base <- @c:base at @s
    @multiplier <- class-table row where class is @c:class
                                   column is @s
    @base × @multiplier (integer-rounded)
]

The class-table is configuration. Every class runs through one
`adjusted` function. OOP inheritance tempts us to override per class;
the Rosetta Stone commits to data-over-dispatch.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: derived stats `where` base and level suffice

[rpg[derived {character: @c}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return a snapshot of HP/MP/armour/etc computed from
        base stats and level, never from stored fields
        so a level-up that forgets to refresh HP is structurally impossible

    {max-hp: 10 + adjusted(STAMINA) × 5 + @c:level × 10,
     max-mp: 10 + adjusted(INTELLECT) × 5 + @c:level × 5,
     armour: adjusted(STRENGTH) / 2 + adjusted(DEXTERITY) / 4,
     carry-capacity: adjusted(STRENGTH) × 10,
     crit-chance: adjusted(LUCK) / 100.0}
]

## Distinctive Innate idiom: spending guarded by `where` budget and cap

[rpg[spend-point {character: @c, stat: @s, points: @n}]
    @c
        where unspent-points at least @n
        and (base at @s + @n) at most class-cap for (@c:class, @s)
        -> apply spend: decrement unspent, increment base
        otherwise
        -> #over-budget or #over-cap (whichever failed first)
]

Both checks are invariants — same shape databases call constraints.
Spending violates one or the other can never slip through; the
resolver refuses before any mutation.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @c <- [rpg[roll {name: "Rook", class: "warrior", seed: 42}]]
    ;; Deterministic across six languages (LCG constants committed):
    ;; STR base=16 adj=24 (1.5x warrior), STA base=4 adj=5 (1.3x)
    ;; level=1 → HP = 10 + 5×5 + 1×10 = 45

    [rpg[level-up {character: @c}]]
    [rpg[level-up {character: @c}]]
    [rpg[spend-point {character: @c, stat: "strength", points: 5}]]
    [rpg[spend-point {character: @c, stat: "stamina", points: 3}]]

    -> [rpg[derived {character: @c}]]
    ;; level=3, STR=21 (adj=31), STA=7 (adj=9)
    ;; HP = 10 + 9×5 + 3×10 = 85
]
